./" Manpage for bfs
.TH man 1 "07 Feb 2026" "1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
	bfs - breadth-first search
.SH SYNOPSIS
	bfs {OPTIONS} dirname
	bfs {OPTIONS} cmd *args
.SH DESCRIPTION
	bfs is a wrapper for executing commands with breadth-first searched arguments.
.br
	If only one argument is supplied, it will search for a directory and cd there.
.br
	Otherwise, it will take the first argument as a literal command, search for the other arguments, and execute the created command.
.br
	If the first arg is not a command, bfs can search for it too.
.SH NOTES
	Use _ before an argument to match it literally, e.g. bfs mv old _new.
.br
	Use __ if you need to match something with an underscore (so bfs ___pycache__ with three underscores if you want to go there for whatever reason)
.SH OPTIONS
	-h explore hidden directories (bfsh)
.br
	-r start from root (bfsr)
.br
	-f explore to maxdepth and cd to last used match (bfsf)
.br
	-t find directories starting with argument (bfst)
.br
	-i infinite maxdepth (bfsi)
.br
	-s find directories containing argument (bfss)
.br
	-d only match directories, not files (bfsd)
.SH EXAMPLES
	bfs src				starting from pwd, go to closest directory named src
.br
	bfs -tf cs          	starting from pwd, goes to most recently used directory starting with cs
.br
	bfs -rs vi .py			starting from root, edit closest file containing .py with vim
.br
	bfs git _add main.c		starting from pwd, git add closest file named main.c
